1
00:00:15,170 --> 00:00:18,550
Hello everyone, welcome back to our listening course.

2
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:21,190
Today we're going to continue to learn about

3
00:00:21,190 --> 00:00:24,410
multiple choice questions in section 3, which is

4
00:00:24,410 --> 00:00:27,470
considered as the most difficult question in IELTS

5
00:00:27,470 --> 00:00:28,330
listening tests.

6
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:31,090
In this lesson we are going to review

7
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:33,690
the format and key features of multiple choice

8
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:36,610
questions in the IELTS listening test part 3,

9
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:40,270
understand and apply effective steps to answer multiple

10
00:00:40,270 --> 00:00:43,690
choice questions including reading and predicting question topics,

11
00:00:44,170 --> 00:00:47,810
underlining keywords, tracking answer options, and taking effective

12
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:48,170
notes.

13
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:51,990
Recognize common mistakes such as not taking notes,

14
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:55,770
choosing answers based solely on keywords without context,

15
00:00:56,170 --> 00:00:58,430
and missing important signposting words.

16
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:02,490
And finally, apply practical tips to improve accuracy

17
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:07,330
including comparing speakers opinions in tables, spotting paraphrases,

18
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:09,490
and eliminating distractions.

19
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:12,010
By the end of this lesson you'll feel

20
00:01:12,010 --> 00:01:14,730
more confident to tackle multiple choice questions in

21
00:01:14,730 --> 00:01:16,890
part 3 of the IELTS listening test.

22
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:19,630
So let's get started.

23
00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:22,750
The steps for multiple choice questions in section

24
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:25,410
3 are not much different from those in

25
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:26,010
section 2.

26
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:28,290
There are only a few differences in the

27
00:01:28,290 --> 00:01:30,150
pre and while listening.

28
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:32,510
Step 1.

29
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:35,770
Before listening you have to read questions to predict 

30
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:37,610
the structure of a listening section.

31
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:40,930
In section 3 you'll hear a conversation between

32
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:43,910
two or three people, often between a student

33
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:46,770
and a teacher talking about academic topics.

34
00:01:47,990 --> 00:01:48,890
Step 2.

35
00:01:49,790 --> 00:01:52,090
You have to underline the keywords in the

36
00:01:52,090 --> 00:01:53,530
questions and the options.

37
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:57,350
Underlining the keywords helps you to locate which

38
00:01:57,350 --> 00:01:59,490
part the speakers are talking about.

39
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:02,390
Guys, pay attention to the keywords that help

40
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:04,290
you differentiate between the options.

41
00:02:05,010 --> 00:02:07,789
Moreover, the keywords in the questions help you

42
00:02:07,789 --> 00:02:10,750
realize whether you need to focus on a

43
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:13,890
person's response or an agreement between two people.

44
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:15,610
Step 3.

45
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:18,870
While listening please move your mouse cursor or

46
00:02:18,870 --> 00:02:20,490
pen to the option being mentioned.

47
00:02:21,210 --> 00:02:23,790
If the question asks you about a person's

48
00:02:23,790 --> 00:02:26,190
opinion, you only have to focus on what

49
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:27,630
that person is saying.

50
00:02:28,230 --> 00:02:30,170
You should take notes next to the option

51
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:32,390
because there will be distractors there.

52
00:02:32,990 --> 00:02:35,510
For questions that require you to seek an

53
00:02:35,510 --> 00:02:38,150
agreement between two people, you should draw a

54
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:40,570
table in which the number of columns corresponds

55
00:02:40,570 --> 00:02:41,910
to the number of speakers.

56
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:44,690
Please note that the options might not come

57
00:02:44,690 --> 00:02:47,310
in order as the information in the recording.

58
00:02:48,550 --> 00:02:49,450
Step 4.

59
00:02:50,250 --> 00:02:52,690
After listening you have to choose the correct

60
00:02:52,690 --> 00:02:55,690
answer and never leave your answer sheet blank.

61
00:02:56,510 --> 00:02:58,730
All right, now let's move to the practice

62
00:02:58,730 --> 00:02:59,150
section.

